Viddaric wrote:

 > My opinion about peaceful/hostile mines:
 >
 > Most of the time, you enter the mines relatively early anyway, so a
 > YASD due to a GWTWOD isn't a major tragedy.

That's true if you only look at that one particular game. But once you
start doing streaks, having one of those end by a wand-zapping monster
is majorly annoying (personal experience).

 > I prefer the hostile mines, because XP is really valuable early on,

You can usually find enough hostiles in the main branch to level up.

Peaceful mines not only means no wand-zapping monsters, but you also
get to Minetown and its shops quickly and safely. This can yield food,
a bag, a magic lamp or even a wand of wishing, also an altar for BUC
testing or sacrificing. Without much danger to your character.

 > and you can take down enemies much easier if you don't have to wait
 > for your pet to do it.

Letting your pet kill peacefuls doesn't take that much longer, but is
safe for you. And it lets your pet grow up which is important.

If you do all the kills yourself, your pet will not grow up and can't
help you later when you encounter more difficult monsters.

 > Plus it lowers the risk of losing your pet to a lucky hit by an
 > enemy.

Only relevant with dwarvish mattocks in my experience. So let your pet
grow up first by killing all the G and it will survive the occasional
h with a mattock easily.

 > And it means that a bunch of the gnomes and dwarves aren't replaced
 > by other, nastier things.

I've played two Law Dwa recently (Arc and Cav). For both, the mines
were a complete *cakewalk*.

 > Usually, my strategy is to take a quick delve into the mines and
 > grab what I need [...] then return to the mazes of menace until I
 > get reflection or MR and poison resistance, then go back to the
 > mines and loot them for all they're worth. Most of the time, it
 > works like a charm.

It's a good strategy which I use for hostile mines myself.

        /stth



